= Epistemology — Technical =

== Normative structure ==
Epistemology is partly normative: it asks not only which cognitive states people actually have, but which beliefs, credences, inquiries, or methods are epistemically permitted, required, justified, rational, or knowledge-producing.

This distinguishes epistemology from a purely descriptive psychology of belief formation.

== Internalism / externalism ==
Internalism can concern access, mental-state dependence, or reasons available to reflection.

Externalism includes theories where reliability, causal history, environmental safety, proper function, or modal relations to truth affect epistemic standing without full reflective access.

The distinction cuts across theories of both [[Justification/Technical|justification]] and [[Knowledge/Technical|knowledge]].

== Structure of justification ==
Foundationalism posits non-inferentially justified/basic states that terminate chains of epistemic dependence.

Coherentism emphasizes mutual support and systemic fit.

Infinitism allows non-repeating infinite chains of reasons.

Hybrid and contextual theories combine structural elements or make standards sensitive to inquiry context.

== Evidentialism and reliabilism ==
Evidentialist theories constrain rational belief by evidence.

Process reliabilism can be expressed schematically as requiring belief-forming process types with sufficiently high truth ratios in the relevant environment/reference class.

The reference-class and generality problems show that "reliable process" requires substantive theory rather than a trivial frequency calculation.

== Formal epistemology ==
Formal epistemology studies:
* probabilistic coherence;
* conditionalization;
* scoring rules;
* epistemic utility;
* belief revision;
* epistemic modal logic;
* confirmation;
* disagreement;
* social learning.

The mathematical formalism does not settle philosophical questions about what propositions, priors, evidence, utilities, or agents should be modeled.

== Social epistemology ==
Technical social-epistemic problems include:
* aggregation of testimony;
* network polarization;
* epistemic dependence;
* expert disagreement;
* collective belief;
* group knowledge;
* incentive-compatible truth seeking;
* institutional reliability.

These problems connect epistemology with game theory, economics, sociology, statistics, and information science.

== Naturalized epistemology ==
Naturalized approaches incorporate empirical psychology, cognitive science, and evolutionary/scientific accounts of cognition into epistemological explanation.

Strong naturalization proposals attempt to replace or transform traditional normative epistemology; weaker approaches use empirical findings to constrain viable epistemic norms.

